nt to say knows that because the  virus doesn't think but it makes its  message something that the cell can can  use and it also is incredibly adaptable  this flexibility mutability of HIV is  part of what's made it so hard to block  but as a model of efficient spread  that's a learning for us as we think  about introducing a new health  innovation into a system it overcomes  resistance cells have evolved to  maintain the status quo the virus has  evolved to overcome mechanisms of  maintaining the status quo so it can  continue in the process of bringing in  total change and something totally new  to this existing system then it  integrates into the DNA that means it  becomes part of what the cell does and  it's no longer knew this is just part of  this system forever and then as I  described in this video it spreads on  existing networks to other similar  systems going through the same process  over and over so when we have something  new a new vaccine a maybe a more for  example depo provera is another one  we've studied it's a long-acting birth  control breastfeeding exclusive  breastfeeding reduced time to treatment  for patients with a certain kind of  heart attack lots of different kinds of  health innovations that we want to bring  in something totally new into an  existing health system we think that we  can learn from a virus that has evolved  to be very very good at spreading to  help us introduce change that could  actually save people's lives thank you
 

